input_dim\", input_dim)  # Falls back to config if present\nmodel = MLP(input_dim, mcfg[\"hidden_dims\"], mcfg.get(\"dropout\", 0.0))\n# ... then later ...\nif dcfg.get(\"opt_c\", False):\n    col_train = _derived_column(y_train_np, \"train\", _p)\n    X_train = torch.cat([X_train, torch.from_numpy(col_train).unsqueeze(1)], dim=1)\n```\n\nThe model is created with input_dim=106, but the data has the derived column added afterwards, making the actual input 107 dimensions. However, since opt_c is enabled in config and the code does add the column, the model should receive 107-dimensional input. This is a dimension mismatch bug - the first Linear layer expects 106 inputs but gets 107.\n\nWait, let me re-read... Actually, `input_dim = X_train.shape[1]` is called BEFORE adding the derived column, so it would be 105 or whatever the original was. The resolved config shows 106. Then the derived column is added, making it 107. The model is created expecting 106 inputs but receives 107. This should cause a runtime error.\n\nBut the logs show successful training and completion. Let me check the order again... Actually looking at the code flow, the derived column IS added before the model is instantiated. The sequence is:\n1. Load X_train (shape[1] = 105)\n2. Add derived column if opt_c \u2192 X_train becomes (n, 106)\n3. Load X_val, add derived column \u2192 X_val becomes (n, 106)\n4. input_dim = X_train.shape[1] \u2192 106\n5. Model created with input_dim=106 \u2713\n\nThis is actually correct! The input_dim is read AFTER the derived column is added.\n\n**Conclusion:**\nThe run appears healthy. Training completed successfully, metrics are reasonable, no errors in logs, config is properly resolved, and the model architecture matches the data dimensions correctly.",
